#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161713 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/id #uuid "1fdcc887-4c52-5d91-ab28-63a7dce9fe5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12462" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\n- $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = f(0,0) = 0.28.$ Likewise, \n\n$$\nP(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0) = f_X(0) \\cdot f_Y(0) = 0.4 \\cdot 0.7 = 0.28. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =0.12=0.4\\cdot 0.3={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.42=0.6\\cdot 0.7={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.18=0.6\\cdot 0.3={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161714 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/id #uuid "4e12d6b6-5771-5e54-8f16-7899f82d90bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12463" :question/problem "The marginal mass functions of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(4,2).$\n\n| | $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | |  | | $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.55$ | $0.45$ | |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |  |  |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.55$ | $0.45$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161715 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/id #uuid "1875d098-95f6-50a7-b069-56865207b3dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12464" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.24$ | $0.06$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.56$ | $0.14$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | |  | | $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.8$ | $0.2$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.8$ | $0.2$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\n- $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = f(0,0) = 0.24.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0) = f_X(0) \\cdot f_Y(0) = 0.3 \\cdot 0.8 = 0.24. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =0.56=0.7\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(0,1) & =0.06=0.3\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.14=0.7\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c87d5ec5-3d8c-54c7-b0ab-60bab136c6e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159261} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd6d82f-1e75-51bf-82bf-1762017ae417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159262} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf29bcc-9c9f-535f-b523-82c56ea1f7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159263} {:answer/id #uuid "4add272b-ba87-5e76-b1c9-a40dd10069d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159264} {:answer/id #uuid "7472efd8-bdf2-54fe-94ed-5f38bdf9ffba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159265}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb17789-20f2-52ef-abc7-01c94f0d6165" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159260} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bddcdc3-0117-5789-a3ad-ba6aafc6b104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383377} {:answer/id #uuid "a1249cf7-75f0-5961-81c3-1869c734e389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383378} {:answer/id #uuid "efd31bc8-a127-522a-8023-054d96b8314f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383379} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f97e0c-20e3-563b-8b65-319653d528c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383380} {:answer/id #uuid "439ce31e-29f2-52ad-8058-a3a0cdd5a72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bddcdc3-0117-5789-a3ad-ba6aafc6b104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383377} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa5d990-d2c9-53e1-986d-feac4f9aa275" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25122d3d-9cb1-5f5e-b327-d0566bb4de5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-136853" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.6.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.75\\cdot 0.8=0.6.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.05=0.25\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.15=0.75\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(0,1) & =0.20=0.25\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121b188d-e60e-512f-85fa-df11d8dd8fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278757} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0f8673-ce7b-514c-821d-99a08c58c384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278996} {:answer/id #uuid "4e156a14-a2d1-589a-8e6c-d7d355641669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279299} {:answer/id #uuid "b942317b-4cdd-5133-86a0-72edd836c45d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280222} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f00225-d1b5-51c3-b4d3-577d7c1a4eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e0f8673-ce7b-514c-821d-99a08c58c384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278996} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfb66dd7-ab41-588b-830e-54e330e9a934" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b10e877-0140-5f79-97b2-25494c500279" :question/math-academy-id "q-162744" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | |  | | $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.6.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.7\\cdot 0.8=0.56.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=1)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf75ab2-772c-57da-b308-392f0140c5d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278707} {:answer/id #uuid "236e1c39-9749-57ed-ad04-dfcf440292ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278909} {:answer/id #uuid "5f819bdf-1c20-593f-83ef-4d1c6dd3cd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279468} {:answer/id #uuid "867cb1fc-c66f-5ce5-bf7f-8b64eb519bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279792} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f4d654-5df4-51ef-9432-ac240b22f91a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f819bdf-1c20-593f-83ef-4d1c6dd3cd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279468} :answer-field/id #uuid "e308b1a0-ff07-540c-b063-07bba0eb3e55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b24b5d8-591a-5107-953f-3194f9f2c074" :question/math-academy-id "q-163605" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{24}$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | |  | | $y$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=0,Y=-2)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=-2)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=-2)=f(0,-2)=\\frac{1}{4}.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=-2)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2)=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =\\frac{5}{12}=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(3,-2) & =\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2) & ✓ \\\\ f(3,2) & =\\frac{5}{24}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
